WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.310
Timothy Spall is an actor with a filmography
that encompasses everything from the cornerstones

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.170
of Western literature to the most modern-minded
pieces in contemporary cinema — and is studded

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.080
with awards and accolades of every kind. Here
are a few of the films you might have seen

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:14.580
him in.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.880
Spall might play a different character in
a very different setting in Tim Burton's adaptation

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.970
of Sweeney Todd, but Potterheads will likely
notice more than a few similarities with Peter

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:30.640
Pettigrew. His character, Beadle Bamford,
is a wretched, gleeful servant of Alan Rickman's

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.490
sinister Judge Turpin, a man whose power he
clings to in the hopes that some might rub

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:41.100
off on him. He is deeply duplicitous, luring
innocents into Turpin's path through carefully

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.430
deployed secrets and lies, skimming whatever
he can off the top whenever he can get away

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:50.170
with it. And he’s not even the villain of
this story, so much as he is a pitiful also-ran

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.500
with delusions of grandeur.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.580
“Will Beadle Bamford be the judge?”

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.800
“Glad, as always, to oblige my friends and
neighbors.”

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:06.700
Above all of these similarities to Pettigrew,
however, is the sheer quality of Spall's performance.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:12.360
No one could have been as perfectly servile
as he is in the role, nor so sumptuously aware

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:17.330
of his character's impotence. Spall plays
Bamford like a man who thinks he's on the

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:21.020
up-and-up, with an actor's awareness of the
fact that he definitely isn’t.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:26.600
As Winston Churchill in The King’s Speech,
Spall plays a larger-than-life role — yet

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.750
in the context of this movie, it’s not exactly
a starring one. Spall is instead a supporting

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:37.800
player to Colin Firth as King George VI in
this portrayal of the king's struggle to master

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:43.250
his persistent stammer. As the movie winds
on, tensions across Europe begins to brew,

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:48.310
and Spall's Churchill finds himself unable
make the declaration of war against Nazi Germany

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:52.460
by himself. That is a duty that falls to the
King, and the King alone.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:57.840
And so, the training begins to take the King
from his impenetrable stutter to smooth, confident

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.900
recitation. The presence of Spall's Churchill
is a grounding force in the film's final scenes,

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:08.120
in which King George VI accomplishes his goal
and changes the world, with nothing but the

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.560
power of his voice, a microphone, and radio
broadcasting.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.900
A rollicking, ruthless portrayal of art and
what fuels it, the critically-acclaimed biopic

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:23.650
Mr. Turner sees Spall play the painter J.
M. W. Turner. Accounts of the time depicted

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:29.140
Turner as consciously refusing the trappings
of fame, tying himself to ship’s masts to

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:33.890
better paint sea scenes, and never quite fitting
in with his colleagues at the Royal Academy

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:38.599
of Art. But his work was utterly sublime,
capturing the power and violence of nature

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.820
as readily as its grace and tranquility.

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:45.960
He was a deeply complicated figure, and Spall
fills the role like no one else could. In

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:50.349
a way, the actor takes to the role as Turner
himself took to painting — passionately

00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:51.340
and skilfully.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.700
“You’ve always been preoccupied. You’re
too preoccupied for your own good, sir.”

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:01.180
Spall plays another villainous henchman in
Disney’s Enchanted. The film sees Giselle,

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:06.080
a woodland princess from the magical land
of Andalasia, trapped in New York City when

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.420
the wicked Queen Narissa pushes her into a
dimension-spanning portal. Narissa sends forth

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:16.090
her helplessly lovesick minion Nathaniel,
to keep an eye on Giselle and generally do

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:16.700
her bidding.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:21.560
Spall gets to be quite a bit funnier here
than as Peter Pettigrew or Beadle Bamford,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:26.410
and, unlike with those other characters, ultimately
gets to find his redemption. Our last glimpse

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:31.130
of Nathaniel in Enchanted finds him promoting
a self-help book about his experiences, with

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:36.080
a long and ardent line of autograph-seekers
waiting to meet him. Here, Spall proves that

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.110
not all of his servile wretches need to be
totally loathsome — he can make you root

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:40.720
for them too.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.680
Unique among these entries, the BBC series
Timothy Spall at Sea is about the man himself.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:51.790
He and his wife Shane are dedicated sailing
enthusiasts, and this BBC program follows

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:56.110
them on their journeys around the British
coast, aboard their Dutch barge, the Princess

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:00.560
Matilda. Spall is a delightful guide to the
world of galleys and moorings — his passion

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.780
for the freedom of life on the seas shining
through at every moment.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:08.540
Three series have been produced: Somewhere
at Sea, Back at Sea, and All at Sea. Though

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:13.580
Spall's skill and expertise is an enormous
part of the show, the foibles of sea life

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:18.469
are part of it as well. The couple infamously
became lost in 2011, and had to be guided

00:04:18.469 --> 00:04:23.180
back to safety by the authorities of the town
of Sheerness. Nevertheless, Timothy Spall

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:24.020
sails on.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:30.180
Spall teamed up with director Mike Leigh for
All or Nothing, centered around three working-class

00:04:30.189 --> 00:04:35.550
families and the traumas and victories that
define their lives. Spall plays Phil, a father

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:40.660
and taxi driver caught in a web of addiction,
dead-end jobs, and family squabbling that

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:44.400
encompasses roughly a dozen people by the
time the film reaches its end.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:49.240
Spall's character lives a life that edges
on bleak, yet he keeps away from toppling

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:54.240
into true despair — something that, if anything,
makes the character even more heart-rending.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.210
His performance has its feet planted firmly
in moments of extreme dysfunction and steadfast

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:04.259
love, an honesty that catapults All or Nothing
into greatness. It's easy to see why Spall

00:05:04.259 --> 00:05:08.930
is one of Leigh's go-to performers. Together,
they find the eye of the storm that is modern

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:09.960
life in the U.K.

